{"name":"Eyelash Viper","hex":"#f4c54b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"eyelash viper","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A species of venomous pit viper, Bothriechis schlegelii, in the family Viperidae, native to Central and South America."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/eyelash_viper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bothriechis schlegelii","blocks":[{"text":"Bothriechis schlegelii, known commonly as the highland eyelash-pitviper, Schlegel's eyelash-pitviper, eyelash viper or eyelash palm viper, is a species of pit viper in the family Viperidae, native to Colombia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Bothriechis_schlegelii_from_Antioquia_department%2C_Colombia%2C_in_life.jpg/330px-Bothriechis_schlegelii_from_Antioquia_department%2C_Colombia%2C_in_life.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bothriechis_schlegelii"},{"title":"Eyelash","blocks":[{"text":"An eyelash is one of the hairs that grows at the edges of the top and bottom eyelids, spanning outwards and away from the eyes. The lashes grow in up to six layers on each of the upper and lower eyelids. Eyelashes serve to protect the eye from debris, dust, and small particles, and are highly sensitive to touch, thus providing a warning that an object is near the eye, which then reflexively closes or flutters to rid the area of the object. The eyelid margin from which lashes grow is among the most sensitive parts of the human body, with many nerve endings enveloping the roots of the lashes, giving it sensitivity to very light tactile input even at the tips of the lashes, enabling it to trigger the blink reflex when touched. Eyelashes are also an important component of physical attractiveness, with long prominent lashes giving the illusion of large, gazing eyes, and drawing attention to the eyes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Human_eyelashes.jpg/330px-Human_eyelashes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eyelash"},{"title":"Viper","blocks":[{"text":"Vipers are snakes in the family Viperidae, found in most parts of the world, except for Antarctica, Australia, Hawaii, Madagascar, Ireland, and various other isolated islands. All vipers are venomous, and have long, hinged fangs that permit deep envenomation of their prey. Three subfamilies are currently recognized. They are also known as viperids. The name \"viper\" is derived from the Latin word vipera, -ae, also meaning viper, possibly from vivus (\"living\") and parere, referring to the trait viviparity common in vipers like most of the species of Boidae. The earliest known vipers are believed to have diverged from the rest of the clade Caenophidia in the early Eocene."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Viperidae-01.jpg/330px-Viperidae-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viper"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Specimens_of_Bothriechis_schlegelii.jpg/500px-Specimens_of_Bothriechis_schlegelii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Specimens_of_Bothriechis_schlegelii.jpg/1280px-Specimens_of_Bothriechis_schlegelii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Specimens_of_Bothriechis_schlegelii.jpg/500px-Specimens_of_Bothriechis_schlegelii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpecimens_of_Bothriechis_schlegelii.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Juvenile_holotype_of_Bothriechis_schlegeliiZFMK_32554_in_latera